\begin{sample}{(Answer to Problem 30, odd-numbered companion: Problem 29 continued)}
For completeness, the companion bound of Problem 30 is evaluated here as well.

\textbf{Step 1:} Differentiating Eq.~(4) term by term gives $\abs{\Nfl'}\le\frac1\pi\sum_p\sum_{m=1}^3\frac{\log p}{p^{m/2}}$, using $\abs{\cos}\le1$.

\textbf{Step 2:} For $p\in\{2,3,5,7,11,13\}$ the sum is $6.568$, so the bound is $2.091$.

\textbf{Step 3:} The slope of the smooth part is $\Nsm'(800)=\frac1{2\pi}\log\frac{800}{2\pi}=0.771$.

\[
\boxed{\abs{\Nfl'}\le2.09\quad\text{vs}\quad\Nsm'(800)=0.771}
\]
The bound exceeds the slope, so monotonicity is \emph{not guaranteed}. However, the bound assumes that all cosines are $\pm1$ simultaneously, which is a worst case, so it does not prove that the equation is root-ambiguous. With 150 primes (the root-ambiguous row of Table~IV) the worst-case bound is far larger still, which makes the observed ambiguity unsurprising.
\end{sample}
